  [FAIL] V1 [VERDICT] the saturated branch is a NUMERICAL HAZARD that has been silently affecting this programme's committed solves   (it has not.  The degeneracy exists only in the CARRIER reading (M3: the AQUAL-on-total-potential reading has Sigma_par = 1 exactly at saturation, and QUMOND is linear), and no committed script solves the carrier equation off spherical symmetry (M4).  The liability is real and was worth checking, but it has never been drawn on)
  [FAIL] V2 [VERDICT] the saturated branch is a PHYSICAL EFFECT the programme has been missing in the regime it cannot explain -- cluster cores   (it is not.  Cluster cores as the repository measures them are DEEP on the unsaturated branch (R3: max s = 0.909 against s_sat = 2.540); the branch only switches on inside ~5 kpc once a BCG is added, inside the audit's innermost radius; the ceiling is unchanged by flattening (O5); the excess/ceiling stays 4.31x; and the 9-sigma lensing-shape failure lives 15x below s_sat (O6))
  [FAIL] V3 [VERDICT] the saturated branch is a FORMAL defect with NO observable consequence anywhere   (not quite.  It IS well posed -- strictly convex, unique to 1e-10, Lipschitz to 0.5% over three decades -- and it is invisible in clusters and in vertical structure (1.80 Msun/pc^2 against a 4 error bar).  But solving the non-spherical equation instead of applying the algebraic law moves a disc's inner rotation curve by 2.72 km/s, one-signed and mesh-converged: small, real, and not zero)
  [PASS] V4 [VERDICT] the saturated branch is WELL POSED as a variational inequality -- the pathology L30 and L33 identified is a loss of classical regularity and of the cubic action, not a loss of existence, uniqueness or continuous dependence   (Sigma_par = +infinity is a CONSTRAINT (|grad phi| <= C a0), not a degeneracy: the energy stays convex, the minimiser exists and is unique, and the map data -> solution is Lipschitz.  What genuinely fails is C^2 regularity at the free boundary -- and the SIGN, since a non-monotone continuation (Sigma_par < 0) WOULD be ill posed, which is exactly what the bounded-boost theorem already forbids)

